<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enguerrand VII de Coucy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1340">1340</a>–<a href="/years/1397">1397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Enguerrand de Coucy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enguerrand VII de Coucy was a French nobleman and soldier, lord of one of the great baronies of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>. He led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> companies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where in 1384 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> and sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a> in 1396 and died a prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enguerrand-vii-de-coucy.jpg" alt="Enguerrand VII de Coucy" width="320" height="555" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rs-nourse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Enguerrand_de_Coucy,_1st_Earl_of_Bedford,_KG.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Enguerrand, also known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as Ingelram de Coucy, became Lord of Coucy when his father Enguerrand VI died during the campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346. His inheritance was the Château de Coucy, with 150 towns and villages, forests and ponds, in the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>. His mother, Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, eldest daughter of Leopold I, Duke of Austria, died in 1349 in a wave of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>. He first fought the English at fifteen, and in 1358, at eighteen, he helped lead the suppression of the peasant revolt known as the Jacquerie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1359 he was one of forty hostages given for the release of King John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in November 1360. He spent five years at the English court, where the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart">Jean Froissart</a> wrote that he was in great favour with both the French and the English. On 27 July 1365 he married Isabella, daughter of Edward III, who chose him for love; he recovered his family's lands in northern England, was freed as a hostage without ransom, and was later made Earl of Bedford and a Knight of the Garter. Back in France in 1368 he granted freedom to 22 towns and villages of his war-damaged estate.</p><p>Coucy kept faith with both the French king and his English father-in-law through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, serving as captain, envoy and mediator, until he resigned his English honours on the accession of Richard II in 1377. In 1375 he hired Free Companies, one led by Owain Lawgoch, to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands he claimed through his mother, but the expedition failed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in the Gugler War. He became Governor of Brittany in 1380, Grand Butler and Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, twice refused to be Constable of France, and joined the Barbary Crusade of 1390.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At Nicopolis on 28 September 1396 Coucy advised a pause to regroup but was overruled by younger knights, and the crusaders were routed. Captured, he was made to watch the beheading of hundreds of prisoners, then marched 350 km to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> and held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> for ransom. Weakened by wounds and hardship, he died there of plague on 18 February 1397 and was buried at the Abbey of Villeneuve near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>. He left no legitimate son, and after disputes over the succession the barony passed to the French crown in 1405. Barbara Tuchman made his life the theme of A Distant Mirror (1978).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enguerrand VII de Coucy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1384 the French captain <b>Enguerrand de Coucy</b> sold it to the Republic of Florence, which ruled it for the rest of its history.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-arezzo">Commune of Arezzo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Bursa, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.19,29.08&amp;year=1377" aria-label="Bursa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5265 726 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Enguerrand VII de Coucy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5715 996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5715 996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.19,29.08&amp;year=1377">What was here in 1377? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
